    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>A public claims adjuster is a licensed professional who represents the policyholder, not the insurance company, in a property insurance claim.</li>
<li>In Texas, public adjusters are licensed and regulated by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) under Insurance Code Chapter 4102.</li>
<li>Fees are set in a written, TDI-approved contract and capped by law at 10% of the insurance settlement, and you can cancel within 72 hours of signing.</li>
<li>The role is most useful on large, complicated, delayed, underpaid, or denied claims. Small, fairly paid claims often do not need one.</li>
<li>A public adjuster cannot guarantee a result, cannot act as your contractor on the same claim, and is not a substitute for an attorney.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>A hailstorm rolls across Dallas County, a pipe lets go in the attic, or a kitchen fire fills the house with smoke. A day or two later you are on the phone with your insurance company, and somewhere in that process a question comes up: <strong>what is a public claims adjuster</strong>, and is it someone you actually need?</p>

<p>The short answer is that a public claims adjuster is a licensed claims professional who works for you, the policyholder. The longer answer, which this guide covers, includes how that role differs from the adjuster your insurer sends, what the work involves day to day, how Texas law controls fees and contracts, and how to decide whether hiring one makes sense for your claim. It is written for homeowners and business owners in Dallas, Dallas County, and nearby North Texas cities, and it is general information, not legal advice.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Are-Public-Adjusters-Worth-It-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster holding a clipboard while a couple points to a water-damaged ceiling inside their home" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Much of a public adjuster’s work happens inside the home, tracing damage the first inspection may not have captured.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What is a public claims adjuster?</h2>

<p>“Public claims adjuster,” “public insurance adjuster,” and “public adjuster” all describe the same job. The word <em>public</em> simply means the adjuster serves members of the public who hold insurance policies, rather than an insurance company.</p>

<p>Texas defines and regulates the profession in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. A public adjuster must hold a license from the Texas Department of Insurance, must use a contract with TDI-approved terms, and must display the phrase “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY” in that contract. That phrase sums up the role: the public adjuster’s duty runs to the person who owns the policy.</p>

<p>Public adjusters handle first-party property claims, meaning claims you make on your own policy for damage to your own home, rental property, or business. Typical losses include hail and wind damage to roofs, water damage from plumbing failures, fire and smoke damage, freeze damage, and commercial property losses. They do not handle auto injury claims or liability claims against someone else.</p>

<h2>The three types of adjusters you may meet</h2>

<p>Part of the confusion around this topic is that three different people can carry the title “adjuster” on the same claim. Here is how they compare.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Adjuster type</th><th>Who hires them</th><th>Who they represent</th><th>Typical role on your claim</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Staff (company) adjuster</td><td>Your insurance company, as an employee</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Investigates, writes the estimate, and applies policy terms for the carrier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independent adjuster</td><td>Your insurer, on contract (often through an adjusting firm)</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Same function as a staff adjuster; common after big storms when claim volume spikes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public claims adjuster</td><td>You, under a written contract</td><td>You, the policyholder</td><td>Documents the loss, prepares the claim, and communicates with the insurer on your behalf</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>None of this means the insurer’s adjuster is acting in bad faith. Many company and independent adjusters are careful and fair. The point is that they have a client, and it is the insurance company. When the estimate looks short or a denial letter does not make sense, a public adjuster is the only adjuster in the process whose job is to look at the claim from your side. For a deeper side-by-side on storm claims, see <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims-in-texas/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>What a public claims adjuster actually does</h2>

<p>The job is less about arguing and more about building a complete, well-supported claim. On a typical file the work includes:</p>

<h3>Reading the policy</h3>
<p>The declarations page, endorsements, deductibles, exclusions, and conditions all affect what is payable. A Dallas homeowners policy may have a separate wind/hail deductible expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit, a roof payment schedule, or a cosmetic damage exclusion. A commercial policy may include business income, equipment breakdown, or ordinance or law coverage. Knowing these terms up front shapes everything that follows.</p>

<h3>Inspecting and documenting the damage</h3>
<p>This means walking the roof, attic, exterior, and every affected room; photographing and measuring damage; checking soft metals and mechanical units for storm marks; and noting conditions that point to cause and date. Tools such as Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging are common in this work because they produce records an insurer can review line by line.</p>

<h3>Writing a detailed estimate</h3>
<p>A public adjuster prepares a room-by-room, line-item estimate of what it should cost to repair or replace the damage with materials of like kind and quality. That estimate is compared to the insurer’s estimate so differences can be discussed item by item, rather than as a single disputed total.</p>

<h3>Preparing and presenting the claim</h3>
<p>Photos, estimates, invoices, contents inventories, and any required proof of loss are organized into a claim package and sent to the insurer. The public adjuster then handles the follow-up: meetings, re-inspections, document requests, and <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> when additional damage is found during repairs.</p>

<h3>Explaining your options</h3>
<p>If the disagreement is about the amount of the loss rather than whether it is covered, many policies include an appraisal clause. A public adjuster can explain how that process works and what it involves. Our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page covers it in more detail.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/How-to-Find-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner sitting on a sofa with a clipboard and phone, a collapsed ceiling visible behind them" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>After a loss, many owners spend hours on calls and paperwork. A public adjuster can take on much of that workload.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What a public claims adjuster cannot do</h2>

<p>Knowing the limits matters as much as knowing the job description. Under Texas rules, a public adjuster:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cannot guarantee an outcome.</strong> Coverage and payment depend on the policy and the facts. Be cautious of anyone who promises a specific result.</li>
<li><strong>Cannot be your contractor on the same claim.</strong> Texas conflict-of-interest rules bar a public adjuster from participating in the repair or reconstruction of property on a claim they are adjusting.</li>
<li><strong>Cannot give legal advice or sue on your behalf.</strong> Questions about bad faith, lawsuits, or deadlines to file suit belong with a Texas attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Cannot keep you from talking with your own insurer.</strong> You remain the policyholder and stay involved in decisions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How public adjusters are paid in Texas</h2>

<p>Most public adjusters work on a percentage of the insurance settlement on the claim, so there is typically no upfront bill. Texas law adds important protections:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</li>
<li>If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the adjuster cannot take a percentage fee and is limited to reasonable hourly compensation.</li>
<li>You can rescind the contract within 72 hours of signing.</li>
</ul>

<p>Because the fee is a percentage, it is worth thinking about whether the claim is large or disputed enough that professional help is likely to make a difference. For a simple claim that is already being paid fairly, the answer may be no, and a good public adjuster will tell you that. At Dallas Public Adjuster, that conversation happens before any contract is signed.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Why the role matters for Dallas claims</h2>

<p>North Texas weather produces claims that are rarely as simple as they first look. Dallas County sits in the southern Plains hail belt, with NOAA storm records showing hail reports peaking in April and May, a long March-to-June season, and a smaller bump in October. The county has also seen severe straight-line winds, such as the May 28, 2024 storm in which the National Weather Service found winds up to 95 mph in Garland, tornadoes including the December 26, 2015 EF4 that tracked through Garland and Rowlett, flash flooding in August 2022, and hard freezes like Winter Storm Uri in February 2021.</p>

<p>Those events tend to create the kinds of disputes where a policyholder’s representative is useful:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail and wind roof claims</strong> on brick-veneer homes with composition shingle roofs, where the question is often repair versus full replacement, or storm damage versus wear. See our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage</strong> that spreads into walls, subfloors, and cabinets, including in older pier-and-beam homes in neighborhoods like Lakewood, the M Streets, and Oak Cliff.</li>
<li><strong>Fire and smoke losses</strong> where contents inventories run to hundreds of items.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial claims</strong> on flat or low-slope TPO and modified-bitumen roofs, apartments, and warehouses, where policies are larger and business income may be involved.</li>
<li><strong>Underpaid or denied claims</strong> where the insurer’s estimate is far below contractor pricing, or a denial cites wear and tear or an exclusion. Our pages on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> explain those situations.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a claim usually works with a public adjuster</h2>

<p>Every claim is different, but the process usually follows a similar path:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Initial conversation and policy review.</strong> You share what happened, the policy, and any estimates or letters you already have. This is the point to decide whether help is likely to be worthwhile.</li>
<li><strong>Contract.</strong> If you move forward, you sign a TDI-compliant contract that states the fee. You have 72 hours to change your mind.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection and documentation.</strong> The property is inspected in detail and the damage is photographed, measured, and recorded.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate and claim package.</strong> A line-item estimate and supporting documents are prepared and sent to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Communication with the insurer.</strong> Meetings, re-inspections, document requests, and discussion of differences between estimates.</li>
<li><strong>Resolution.</strong> The insurer issues its decision and payment under the policy terms. Under replacement cost coverage, recoverable depreciation is often released after repairs are completed.</li>
</ol>

<p>Throughout, Texas’s <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prompt Payment of Claims law</a> sets timelines for the insurer. It generally has 15 days after notice of a claim to acknowledge it, begin its investigation, and request information, and 15 business days after receiving everything it requested to accept or reject the claim, with up to 45 more days if it notifies you it needs more time. Those deadlines can be extended after a declared weather catastrophe.</p>

<h2>Common myths about public adjusters</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>“Only huge claims need one.”</strong> Size matters, but complexity and disagreement matter more. A mid-sized roof claim with a large gap between estimates can be a good candidate.</li>
<li><strong>“My insurer will be upset.”</strong> Insurers work with public adjusters routinely. Representation is a legal, regulated part of the Texas claims process.</li>
<li><strong>“It will slow everything down.”</strong> A well-organized claim can reduce back-and-forth, although adding a detailed estimate may lead to more discussion before the claim is resolved.</li>
<li><strong>“It’s the same as hiring a lawyer.”</strong> It is not. Public adjusters work within the claim; attorneys handle legal disputes and litigation.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/When-Is-It-Too-Late-to-Hire-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Hand signing claim papers next to a model house and calculator, with a burned house visible through the window" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Read any contract or settlement document carefully before signing. Texas public adjuster contracts include a 72-hour right to rescind.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to choose a public claims adjuster in Dallas</h2>

<p>If you decide to hire help, take a few minutes to vet the person and the company:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Verify the license</strong> using TDI’s <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/agent/agent-lookup.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">online license lookup</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Read the contract.</strong> It should state the fee, include the 72-hour rescission right, and say “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.”</li>
<li><strong>Ask how they document claims.</strong> You want detailed photos, measurements, and a line-item estimate, not just a phone call to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Ask about relevant experience</strong> with your type of loss, whether that is hail, water, fire, or a commercial property.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for red flags:</strong> pressure to sign immediately, promises of a specific payout, or an offer to also do the repairs.</li>
</ul>

<p>TDI’s <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/public-adjusters.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">consumer page on public adjusters</a> is a good, neutral overview to read before you sign anything.</p>

<h2>Final thoughts</h2>

<p>Knowing what a public claims adjuster is does not mean you have to hire one. It means you understand your options before a claim becomes stressful. If your loss is small and the insurer is treating it fairly, you may be able to handle it yourself with good photos and a careful read of the estimate. If the damage is extensive, the numbers do not add up, or the claim has stalled, it may be time to get a second set of eyes. Our article on <a href="/knowing-when-to-call-a-public-adjuster/">knowing when to call a public adjuster</a> walks through the timing in more detail.</p>

